In this lesson we'll look at the building blocks of probability: experiments, sample spaces, and events. A random experiment is just a process where you don't know what's going to happen, but you do know what could happen — like flipping a coin, rolling a die, or drawing a card. Each single result is called an outcome, so rolling a four is an outcome. The sample space is the full set of all possible outcomes, so for a standard die the sample space is just one, two, three, four, five, six. An event is any collection of outcomes you care about, and since it's a subset of the sample space, it can be one outcome or many. A single outcome is called an elementary event, and one with more than one outcome is a compound event, like "rolling an even number," which would be two, four, or six. When every outcome is equally likely, the probability of an event is just the number of outcomes in that event divided by the total number of outcomes. And remember, every probability sits between zero and one, and all the probabilities of every outcome always add up to one. Now let's see it in action.